<h3>Evening — Dinner and Downtown After Dark</h3>
<p>As the afternoon heat fades, Palm Springs takes on a different atmosphere.</p>
<p>Restaurants begin filling, outdoor patios become inviting, and downtown offers a pleasant setting for an evening stroll.</p>
<p>For dinner, Johannes is an appealing choice if you're looking for a memorable meal. Chef Johannes Bacher's Austrian-influenced cuisine and signature schnitzel provide something distinctive.</p>
<p>Pomme Frite offers another possibility, with a European bistro atmosphere and Belgian-inspired dishes.</p>
<p>If your visit falls on a Thursday, check whether VillageFest is taking place along Palm Canyon Drive. The weekly street fair brings additional energy to downtown.</p>
<p>Otherwise, there's no need for elaborate evening plans.</p>
<p>A good dinner and an unhurried walk can be a satisfying conclusion to your first day.</p>
<hr/>
<h2>Day Two: Discover the Desert Beyond Downtown</h2>
<h3>Morning — The Palm Oases of Indian Canyons</h3>
<p>After breakfast, head toward Indian Canyons, part of the ancestral homeland of the Agua Caliente Band of Cahuilla Indians.</p>
<p>The landscape offers a remarkable contrast to the carefully maintained streets and gardens of Palm Springs.</p>
<p>Here, native California fan palms grow in sheltered canyons, surrounded by rugged rock formations and the arid terrain of the desert.</p>
<p>For visitors who want to experience a palm oasis without committing to a lengthy hike, Andreas Canyon is worth considering.</p>
<p>Its relatively easy, approximately one-mile loop provides an opportunity to enjoy the palms, rocky scenery, and flowing water when conditions permit.</p>
<p>Those who prefer a longer adventure can explore additional trails, but there's no need to undertake a strenuous hike to appreciate the character of Indian Canyons.</p>
<p>Allow roughly 90 minutes to two hours for a shorter visit, including time to enjoy the surroundings.</p>
<p>Bring drinking water, wear suitable walking shoes, and check trail conditions and operating schedules before arriving. Summer access can be limited, and temperatures can become dangerously high.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.indian-canyons.com/hours">Check Indian Canyons visitor information</a></p>
<figure class="destination-photo"><img alt="Palm Springs palm oasis" loading="lazy" src="/images/palm-springs-palm-oasis.webp"/></figure>
<p><em>Caption: Tall palms rise against the rugged desert mountains of Palm Springs, illustrating the striking contrast between greenery and arid terrain.</em></p>
<p><em>Credit: Katterinaaa / Pexels. Photograph identified as Palm Springs; specific canyon not established.</em></p>
<h3>Afternoon — From Desert Floor to Mountain Forest</h3>
<p>Few attractions demonstrate the dramatic geography of Palm Springs quite like the Palm Springs Aerial Tramway.</p>
<p>In a relatively short ride, visitors travel from the desert foothills high into the San Jacinto Mountains.</p>
<p>The rotating tram cars provide changing views of the rugged canyon walls and the Coachella Valley far below.</p>
<p>At the upper station, the landscape feels entirely different. Mountain forests replace desert vegetation, and temperatures can be considerably cooler than those in Palm Springs.</p>
<p>Allow approximately two to three hours for the excursion, with additional time for driving, parking, waiting for tram cars, or exploring the mountain trails.</p>
<p>Bring a jacket or extra layer, even when the weather in town feels warm.</p>
<p><a href="https://pstramway.com/tickets/">Check Aerial Tramway tickets and operating information</a></p>
